A single-storey plan needs five rooms, each with a doorway directly to every one of the other four, with no doorway crossing another.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\coordinate (R1) at (0,2.8);
\coordinate (R2) at (2.2,4.0);
\coordinate (R3) at (4.2,2.6);
\coordinate (R4) at (3.4,0.2);
\coordinate (R5) at (0.8,0);
\draw (R1)--(R2);
\draw (R1)--(R3);
\draw (R1)--(R4);
\draw (R1)--(R5);
\draw (R2)--(R3);
\draw (R2)--(R4);
\draw (R2)--(R5);
\draw (R3)--(R4);
\draw (R3)--(R5);
\draw (R4)--(R5);
\fill (R1) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (R2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (R3) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (R4) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (R5) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left] at (R1) {$R_1$};
\node[above] at (R2) {$R_2$};
\node[right] at (R3) {$R_3$};
\node[below right] at (R4) {$R_4$};
\node[below left] at (R5) {$R_5$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Is this graph planar?

